    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f6b23d3b-dd76-5687-8c45-919862fa58e3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{11}\\right)\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{11}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{11}\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=\\frac{\\pi}{11}$. Recall $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} &=2\\frac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=2\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=2\\tan(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is nonzero, and $\\tan(2x)=\\sin(2x)/\\cos(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{11}$ and double the whole angle: $2x=\\frac{2\\pi}{11}$. The result is\n\n$$\n2\\tan\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{11}\\right) .\n$$",
